你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。

每个参考题描述包含：
- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。
- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。
- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。
- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。

参考题目如下：
<reference_puzzle index="1" source="eval" puzzle_id="45a5af55" lines="5-18">
<description>
Build nested square frames from a stack of horizontal color stripes.

Input structure:
- Full-width horizontal stripes of distinct non-bg colors, stacked vertically.
- Each stripe has uniform color and variable height (1-4 rows).

Rule:
- Read stripes bottom to top.
- Bottom stripe becomes the center solid square with side length = stripe height.
- Each subsequent stripe wraps the current shape with a square frame of thickness = stripe height, in that stripe's color.

Key insight: Stripe height doubles as both frame thickness and (for the innermost) center-square side length; output dimensions are fully determined by the stripe heights.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="878187ab" lines="10-24">
<description>
Replace the scattered foreground markers with a bottom-left count rectangle whose two corner diagonals encode the marker counts.

Input structure
- A background grid with exactly two foreground colors used as scattered marker pixels.
- The two foreground colors have distinct pixel counts.

Rule
- Ignore marker positions and use only the two foreground counts.
- Make a 16-by-16 background canvas, fill a bottom-left red rectangle whose height is the smaller count and width is the larger count.
- Paint yellow pixels along the two rising 45-degree diagonals from the rectangle's bottom-left and bottom-right corners.

Key insight
- The marker colors and positions vary between examples; their counts alone determine the output rectangle dimensions.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="5ad8a7c0" lines="10-24">
<description>
Connect the innermost mirrored marker pair(s) by filling their horizontal span.

Input structure
- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.
- Each foreground row contains exactly two foreground pixels mirrored across the vertical center.

Rule
- Find the foreground row pair(s) with the smallest horizontal gap between the two markers.
- Fill every pixel between those two markers in each selected row with the foreground color.
- Leave all other pixels unchanged.

Key insight
- Wider marker pairs are context; the closest marker pair(s) show the unresolved horizontal segment.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="e048c9ed" lines="11-26">
<description>
Add a quantity-color label in the top marker column for each horizontal bar.

Input structure
- The grid has a black background and one non-background marker pixel in the top row.
- Below the marker row, every non-empty source row contains one solid monochrome horizontal bar to the left of the marker column.
- Source rows are staggered every other row.

Rule
- Copy the input grid.
- For each source bar, read its quantity as the bar length.
- Paint the quantity color in the marker column on the same row as the bar:
  2->1, 3->4, 4->9, 5->6.

Key insight
- The grey marker supplies the result column, while each bar supplies a small quantity with a fixed color code.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="fd02da9e" lines="26-41">
<description>
Replace each colored corner marker with its corner-specific inward stencil in the same color.

Input structure
- The grid is 8x8 with a single dominant background color.
- Foreground pixels are marker pixels, each located at one of the four corners.
- A marker's color is carried to the stencil selected by its corner position.

Rule
- Erase each corner marker back to the background.
- Top corner markers become an inward 2x2 block one cell away from the top and side edges.
- Bottom corner markers become the mirrored three-pixel inward bent stencil shown by the bottom-corner examples.

Key insight
- The marker position selects the fixed stencil; the marker color only supplies the paint color, so multiple corners apply independently.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="dce56571" lines="8-23">
<description>
Straighten the single foreground mask into a centered horizontal segment of the same color and length.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color.
- All non-background pixels share one foreground color.
- The number of foreground pixels fits in one centered row of the grid.

Rule:
- Erase the original foreground mask.
- Count its pixels, keep its color, and paint that many pixels as a horizontal segment.
- Place the segment on the middle row with equal background margin on the left and right.

Key insight:
- The original foreground shape is irrelevant except for its color and area; the output is the area normalized into the grid's central horizontal line.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="af726779" lines="17-35">
<description>
Grow an alternating pink/orange triangular trace downward from the horizontal orange seed row.

Input structure:
- The grid has a green background and one horizontal row containing all orange foreground pixels.
- The seed row may contain isolated orange pixels and adjacent orange runs.
- Pink is absent from the input and is used for the first generated row.

Rule:
- Copy the input grid.
- Two rows below the current active row, place a child pixel between each active pair
  whose middle cell in the current row is background.
- Alternate generated row colors between pink and orange, stopping when no children
  can be generated or the next row would leave the grid.

Key insight:
- Only an active-background-active triplet in the current row reproduces; contiguous
  active runs do not produce children through occupied middle cells.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="88bcf3b4" lines="229-241">
<description>
Swing an irregular line segment around a nearby obstacle sprite, preserving its pixel count.

Input structure:
- bg, bar color (straight lines perpendicular to the grid edge, one end on the border), segment color (irregular stroke attached to bar's inner endpoint), obstacle color (sprite near the bar terminal).

Rule:
- Each bar is perpendicular to the edge it touches; its inner (terminal) endpoint has an attached irregular segment.
- The segment detaches and re-routes: it travels from the terminal toward the nearest obstacle, hugs the obstacle's boundary, then exits at a 45° diagonal.
- Total painted pixels equal the original segment length.

Key insight: The segment behaves like a rope swung around the obstacle — attach at the nearest point, trace the skin, exit diagonally, conserving length.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="963c33f8" lines="86-108">
<description>
Drop the columns of the top marker downward, stopping blue-ended columns on grey obstacles and letting maroon-ended columns pass to the bottom.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- The most numerous non-background color forms stationary grey obstacle pixels.
- The other two colors form a single 3x3 top-edge marker whose top two rows
  are maroon and whose bottom row contains maroon or blue control pixels.

Rule
- Erase the top marker.
- Treat each marker column as a vertical drop retaining its three colors.
- A column whose bottom control pixel is blue stops immediately above the
  first grey pixel below that column, or bottom-aligns if none exists.
- Contiguous maroon-ended columns pass through obstacles and bottom-align;
  if such a run has a clear down-right slide from a grey ledge, shift the
  run one column right before bottom-aligning.
- When a maroon-ended drop lands through a grey bar on the bottom edge, clear
  the rest of the marker-width gap in that bottom bar.

Key insight
- The bottom row of the top marker is a control strip: blue cells mean
  "stick to the first obstacle," while maroon cells mean "fall through."
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="902510d5" lines="58-80">
<description>
Keep the largest 8-connected motif and replace the corner/control pixels with a solid corner triangle.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- Exactly one grid corner contains a foreground control pixel; it gives the
  corner from which the triangle is painted.
- There is one unique largest 8-connected monochrome foreground object, the
  motif that remains in the output; all other foreground pixels are controls.
- Every foreground component outside that motif is a singleton control pixel.
- The fill color is the unique control color that appears in at least two
  non-corner control pixels.

Rule
- Start from a blank background canvas and redraw only the largest motif.
- Count the non-corner control pixels; that count is the triangle side.
- Paint the fill color on the right-triangle region where those two distances
  from the marked corner's edges sum to less than the side length.

Key insight
- The scattered singleton controls are counted into a compact triangular
  stack at the marked corner; only the largest motif is not part of the count.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="fe45cba4" lines="72-89">
<description>
Move the left-edge fragment into its same-colored anchor fragment to complete a solid rectangle.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background color.
- Exactly one foreground color is split into two 4-connected objects: one
  touches the left edge, and the other is an anchor away from the left edge.
- The translated left-edge object and the anchor can form one filled rectangle.

